{"type":"user","uuid":"969f81c1-6dd6-4ecf-87cc-908c6030574c","session_id":"89fb7edf-29c1-46f7-b440-f4f18a95580a","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_1d70ed3461444691a19f363c","is_error":false,"content":"/*\n * WiFiAnalyzer\n * Copyright (C) 2018  VREM Software Development <VREMSoftwareDevelopment@gmail.com>\n *\n * This program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify\n * it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by\n * the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or\n * (at your option) any later version.\n *\n * This program is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,\n * but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of\n * MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the\n * GNU General Public License for more details.\n *\n * You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License\n * along with this program.  If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>\n */\n\npackage com.vrem.wifianalyzer.vendor.model;\n\nimport android.content.res.Resources;\nimport android.support.annotation.NonNull;\n\nimport com.vrem.util.FileUtils;\nimport com.vrem.wifianalyzer.R;\n\nimport org.apache.commons.collections4.CollectionUtils;\nimport org.apache.commons.collections4.IterableUtils;\nimport org.apache.commons.collections4.Predicate;\nimport org.apache.commons.collections4.PredicateUtils;\nimport org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils;\n\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\n\nclass VendorDB implements VendorService {\n    private final Resources resources;\n    private final Map<String, List<String>> vendors;\n    private final Map<String, String> macs;\n    private boolean loaded;\n\n    VendorDB(@NonNull Resources resources) {\n        this.resources = resources;\n        this.vendors = new TreeMap<>();\n        this.macs = new TreeMap<>();\n        this.loaded = false;\n    }\n\n    @NonNull\n    @Override\n    public String findVendorName(String address) {\n        String result = getMacs().get(VendorUtils.clean(address));\n        return result == null ? StringUtils.EMPTY : result;\n    }\n\n    @NonNull\n    @Override\n    public List<String> findMacAddresses(String vendorName) {\n        if (StringUtils.isBlank(vendorName)) {\n            return new ArrayList<>();\n        }\n        List<String> results = getVendors().get(vendorName);\n        return results == null ? new ArrayList<>() : results;\n    }\n\n    @NonNull\n    @Override\n    public List<String> findVendors() {\n        return new ArrayList<>(getVendors().keySet());\n    }\n\n    @NonNull\n    @Override\n    public List<String> findVendors(@NonNull String filter) {\n        Predicate<String> predicate =\n            PredicateUtils.anyPredicate(new StringContains(filter), new MacContains(filter));\n        return new ArrayList<>(CollectionUtils.select(getVendors().keySet(), predicate));\n    }\n\n    @NonNull\n    Map<String, List<String>> getVendors() {\n        load(resources);\n        return vendors;\n    }\n\n    @NonNull\n    Map<String, String> getMacs() {\n        load(resources);\n        return macs;\n    }\n\n    private void load(@NonNull Resources resources) {\n        if (!loaded) {\n            loaded = true;\n            String[] lines = FileUtils.readFile(resources, R.raw.data).split(\"\\n\");\n            for (String data : lines) {\n                if (data != null) {\n                    String[] parts = data.split(\"\\\\|\");\n                    if (parts.length == 2) {\n                        List<String> addresses = new ArrayList<>();\n                        String name = parts[0];\n                        vendors.put(name, addresses);\n                        int length = parts[1].length();\n                        for (int i = 0; i < length; i += VendorUtils.MAX_SIZE) {\n                            String mac = parts[1].substring(i, i + VendorUtils.MAX_SIZE);\n                            addresses.add(VendorUtils.toMacAddress(mac));\n                            macs.put(mac, name);\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private class StringContains implements Predicate<String> {\n        private final String filter;\n\n        private StringContains(@NonNull String filter) {\n            this.filter = filter;\n        }\n\n        @Override\n        public boolean evaluate(String object) {\n            return object.contains(filter);\n        }\n    }\n\n    private class MacContains implements Predicate<String> {\n        private final String filter;\n\n        private MacContains(@NonNull String filter) {\n            this.filter = filter;\n        }\n\n        @Override\n        public boolean evaluate(String object) {\n            return IterableUtils.matchesAny(findMacAddresses(object), new StringContains(filter));\n        }\n    }\n\n}\n"}]}}
{"type":"user","uuid":"2f1cfec2-3f9f-44f2-b396-bf0ca580e708","session_id":"89fb7edf-29c1-46f7-b440-f4f18a95580a","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_531c3923c87d483fac576982","is_error":false,"content":"/*\n * WiFiAnalyzer\n * Copyright (C) 2018  VREM Software Development <VREMSoftwareDevelopment@gmail.com>\n *\n * This program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify\n * it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by\n * the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or\n * (at your option) any later version.\n *\n * This program is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,\n * but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of\n * MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the\n * GNU General Public License for more details.\n *\n * You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License\n * along with this program.  If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>\n */\n\npackage com.vrem.wifianalyzer.vendor.model;\n\nimport android.support.annotation.NonNull;\n\nimport org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils;\n\nclass VendorUtils {\n    static final int MAX_SIZE = 6;\n    private static final String SEPARATOR = \":\";\n\n    private VendorUtils() {\n        throw new IllegalStateException(\"Utility class\");\n    }\n\n    @NonNull\n    static String clean(String macAddress) {\n        if (macAddress == null) {\n            return StringUtils.EMPTY;\n        }\n        String result = macAddress.replace(SEPARATOR, \"\");\n        return result.substring(0, Math.min(result.length(), MAX_SIZE)).toUpperCase();\n    }\n\n    @NonNull\n    static String toMacAddress(String source) {\n        if (source == null) {\n            return StringUtils.EMPTY;\n        }\n        if (source.length() < MAX_SIZE) {\n            return \"*\" + source + \"*\";\n        }\n        return source.substring(0, 2)\n            + SEPARATOR + source.substring(2, 4)\n            + SEPARATOR + source.substring(4, 6);\n    }\n\n}\n"}]}}
{"type":"user","uuid":"4a350efe-f51f-451d-9154-f06ccdf827d9","session_id":"89fb7edf-29c1-46f7-b440-f4f18a95580a","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_0bd85dd67709405190d4927f","is_error":false,"content":"/*\n * WiFiAnalyzer\n * Copyright (C) 2018  VREM Software Development <VREMSoftwareDevelopment@gmail.com>\n *\n * This program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify\n * it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by\n * the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or\n * (at your option) any later version.\n *\n * This program is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,\n * but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of\n * MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the\n * GNU General Public License for more details.\n *\n * You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License\n * along with this program.  If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>\n */\n\npackage com.vrem.wifianalyzer.vendor;\n\nimport android.os.Bundle;\nimport android.support.annotation.NonNull;\nimport android.support.annotation.Nullable;\nimport android.support.v4.app.ListFragment;\nimport android.view.LayoutInflater;\nimport android.view.View;\nimport android.view.ViewGroup;\nimport android.widget.SearchView;\nimport android.widget.SearchView.OnQueryTextListener;\n\nimport com.vrem.util.TextUtils;\nimport com.vrem.wifianalyzer.MainContext;\nimport com.vrem.wifianalyzer.R;\n\npublic class VendorFragment extends ListFragment {\n\n    @Nullable\n    @Override\n    public View onCreateView(LayoutInflater inflater, ViewGroup container, Bundle savedInstanceState) {\n        View view = inflater.inflate(R.layout.vendor_content, container, false);\n        VendorAdapter vendorAdapter = new VendorAdapter(getActivity(), MainContext.INSTANCE.getVendorService());\n        setListAdapter(vendorAdapter);\n\n        SearchView searchView = view.findViewById(R.id.vendorSearchText);\n        searchView.setOnQueryTextListener(new Listener(vendorAdapter));\n\n        return view;\n    }\n\n    static class Listener implements OnQueryTextListener {\n        private final VendorAdapter vendorAdapter;\n\n        Listener(@NonNull VendorAdapter vendorAdapter) {\n            this.vendorAdapter = vendorAdapter;\n        }\n\n        @Override\n        public boolean onQueryTextSubmit(String query) {\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        @Override\n        public boolean onQueryTextChange(String newText) {\n            vendorAdapter.update(TextUtils.trim(newText));\n            return true;\n        }\n    }\n\n}\n"}]}}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"624b45c5-b6c5-4831-b5a4-43ba1f039bf6","session_id":"89fb7edf-29c1-46f7-b440-f4f18a95580a","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"624b45c5-b6c5-4831-b5a4-43ba1f039bf6","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see the bug. Let me trace the flow:\n\n1. When the user types in the search box in `VendorFragment`, the `onQueryTextChange` callback is triggered\n2. It calls `vendorAdapter.update(TextUtils.trim(newText))`\n3. Looking at `VendorAdapter`, the `update` method calls `vendorService.findVendors(filter)`\n4. In `VendorDB.findVendors(filter)`, there are two predicates:\n   - `StringContains(filter)` - checks if the vendor name contains the filter\n   - `MacContains(filter)` - checks if any MAC address for that vendor matches\n   \nThe `VendorUtils.clean()` method converts MAC addresses to uppercase via `.toUpperCase()`, so the internal `macs` map stores keys like `00163E`. When a lowercase filter like `00:16:3e` is entered, it gets cleaned to `00163E` and correctly looks up the vendor. However, the `StringContains` and `MacContains` predicates perform case-sensitive substring matching on the vendor name set, which means they won't find matches if the filter case doesn't align with what's stored.\n\nThe real issue is that `filter.toLowerCase()` converts the input to lowercase (`00163e`), but the stored MAC addresses in the data file are in uppercase format. The `IterableUtils.matchesAny()` call then tries to match against `findMacAddresses(object)` where the object is already lowercase, creating a case mismatch that prevents finding the correct vendor. The `MacContains` predicate takes a different approach—it calls `findMacAddresses(object)` to look up MACs associated with a given vendor name, then checks if any of those MACs contain the filter string. The issue is that `object` represents a vendor name from the map keys, not a MAC address, so this logic seems backwards for what it's trying to accomplish. The intended workflow should be: user input gets validated against the 6-character hex prefix format, then used to find matching vendors by looking up their MAC addresses, and finally filtering the vendor list. The predicate logic needs to handle both vendor name matching and MAC address prefix matching, but the current `MacContains` implementation doesn't align with that goal. `findMacAddresses(object)` returns MACs for a vendor name, which doesn't make sense here. Let me trace through `findVendors(String filter)` more carefully — it uses `CollectionUtils.select()` on vendor names, applying a predicate that combines `StringContains` (checks if the vendor name contains the filter) and `MacContains` (which seems to be the problematic part). The issue is that `MacContains` is trying to match MACs against a vendor name, which is backwards. The real problem is in `MacContains.evaluate()`: it calls `findMacAddresses(object)` where `object` is the vendor name, but it should be looking up MACs to validate against, not treating the vendor name as a lookup key. The predicate logic is fundamentally broken because it's searching for a filter string within MAC addresses that belong to the current vendor being evaluated, rather than checking if the filter matches the vendor's MACs directly. The vendor name predicate does a case-sensitive substring check, so \"Cisco\" wouldn't match \"CISCO\". The MAC predicate extracts the first 6 hex characters from the filter, looks up all associated MACs for that vendor prefix, then checks if any of those MACs contain the filter string—which fails because the filter is lowercase while the MACs are uppercase.\n\nI see two distinct problems: the vendor name comparison is case-sensitive, and the MAC address comparison is too. The real issue from the bug report is that hex input like `00:16:3e` doesn't match `00:16:3E` because MAC addresses are stored in uppercase but the filter string isn't normalized.\n\nLooking at the data structure, MACs are stored as uppercase 6-character strings in the database, so when a user enters lowercase hex, the predicate fails to find matches. The fix requires normalizing both the filter and the MACs to uppercase before comparison. I also need to address the vendor name matching, which has the same case-sensitivity problem. I need to make both the vendor name and MAC address comparisons case-insensitive. For the vendor name predicate, I'll convert both strings to lowercase before checking if one contains the other. For the MAC address predicate, I'll normalize the filter to lowercase and then convert each MAC to lowercase before checking for a match, ensuring the comparison works regardless of how the user enters the hex digits. Let me verify this approach against the existing test cases to make sure it handles the expected behavior correctly.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
