{"type":"assistant","uuid":"6f6f83d5-8ac5-40f1-a23d-10240512976d","session_id":"89fb7edf-29c1-46f7-b440-f4f18a95580a","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"6f6f83d5-8ac5-40f1-a23d-10240512976d","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The fix is applied. Let me now create a comprehensive summary of the changes.\n\n## Summary of the Bug Fix\n\n### Bug Description\nMAC vendor search was case-sensitive. Users typing lowercase hex addresses (e.g., `00:16:3e`) got no results even though the same address in uppercase (e.g., `00:16:3E`) would match. Hexadecimal notation is case-insensitive, so the search should be too.\n\n### Root Cause\nIn `VendorDB.findVendors(String filter)`, two inner predicate classes performed case-sensitive `String.contains()` checks:\n1. `StringContains` - used to filter vendor names\n2. `MacContains` - used to filter by MAC address prefix\n\nSince MAC addresses are stored in UPPERCASE (e.g., `00163E`), lowercase input like `00:16:3e` → cleaned to `00163e` wouldn't match the stored uppercase `00163E`.\n\n### Fix\nModified both predicate classes to perform case-insensitive comparison:\n- `StringContains`: Converts both the filter and the vendor name to lowercase before `contains()`\n- `MacContains`: Converts both the filter and each MAC address to lowercase before `contains()`\n\n### Files Changed\n- `app/src/main/java/com/vrem/wifianalyzer/vendor/model/VendorDB.java`\n\nThe build cannot be verified due to network restrictions (Android Gradle Plugin cannot be downloaded from remote repositories). However, the code change is syntactically correct and logically sound.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"09946f5d-28a5-45bb-9611-108d916c1e89","session_id":"89fb7edf-29c1-46f7-b440-f4f18a95580a","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"09946f5d-28a5-45bb-9611-108d916c1e89","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The fix is applied. Here's a summary:\n\n## Bug Fix Summary\n\n### Bug\nMAC vendor search was case-sensitive. Users typing lowercase hex addresses (e.g., `00:16:3e`) got zero results even though the same address in uppercase (`00:16:3E`) matched correctly. Hexadecimal is case-insensitive by definition.\n\n### Root Cause\nIn `VendorDB.findVendors(String filter)`, two inner `Predicate` classes performed case-sensitive `String.contains()` comparisons:\n\n1. **`StringContains`** — filters vendor names by substring match\n2. **`MacContains`** — filters by MAC address prefix match\n\nSince MAC addresses are stored in UPPERCASE in the database (e.g., `00163E`), user input like `00:16:3e` gets cleaned to `00163e` (lowercase) and fails to match the stored uppercase key.\n\n### Fix Applied\n**File:** `app/src/main/java/com/vrem/wifianalyzer/vendor/model/VendorDB.java`\n\nBoth inner predicate classes now normalize to lowercase before comparison:\n\n| Class | Before | After |\n|-------|--------|-------|\n| `StringContains` | `object.contains(filter)` | `object.toLowerCase().contains(filter.toLowerCase())` |\n| `MacContains` | `mac -> mac.contains(filter)` (via `StringContains`) | `mac -> mac.toLowerCase().contains(filter.toLowerCase())` |\n\n### Build Verification\nThe project cannot be built in this environment due to network restrictions (no access to Google/jcenter Maven repos for the Android Gradle Plugin). However, the changes are:\n- Syntactically correct Java\n- Use standard `String.toLowerCase()` which is always available\n- Logically equivalent to the existing working code, just case-insensitive"}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39071,"output_tokens":724,"cache_r